    <link:footnote xlink:type="resource" xlink:label="fn-8" id="fn-8" xml:lang="en" xlink:role="http://www.xbrl.org/2003/role/footnote">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">The information below includes the rate order or document number and the period of amortization, if applicable, for each regulatory asset listed on page 232.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Environmental SIR Reserve/Amortization:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 4610, the Environmental Response Cost ("ERC") Factor is designed to allow Rhode Island Energy to recover its reasonable and prudently incurred costs for evaluation, remediation and clean-up of the sites associated with the Company&#8217;s ownership and/or operation of manufactured gas plants ("MGP"), manufactured gas storage facilities, and MGP related off-site waste disposal locations. The ERC factor is a per-therm charge that reflects the 10-year amortization of environmental response costs.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS158-Pension-Electric:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the Distribution Adjustment Charge (DAC). In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS158-Pension-Gas:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Pension Exp Deferred-Gas:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS158 - NQ Pension &#8211; Electric:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">LIHEAP Enhancement Program - Gas</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4290, R.I.P.U.C. No. 2143, and in accordance with R.I.G.L. &#167; 39-1-27.12, the Company bills to all customers a LIHEAP Enhancement Plan charge approved by the Commission. A LIHEAP Enhancement charge fund is used to account for the combined funds collected through the LIHEAP Enhancement charge from both gas and electric service customers. The State Office of Energy Resource designates to the Company the qualifying customer accounts and the amounts to be credited from the LIHEAP Enhancement fund. The cumulative annual amount of credits applied to customer bills will be limited to an amount no greater than the cumulative aggregate projected LIHEAP Enhancement Plan Charge billed through the end of the current calendar year. Once the aggregate credits applied to customers bills equals the aggregate projected Enhancement Plan charge billed during the calendar year, including interest at the customer deposit interest rate, the Enhancement Plan credits would cease.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%">Standard Offer Service (Commodity):</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 4691, the Company is required to reconcile Standard Offer Service (SOS) revenues and expenses in accordance with the SOS Adjustment Provision, RIPUC No. 2157. This provision requires the Company to reconcile its total cost of purchased power for SOS supply against its total SOS revenue, and the excess or deficiency be refunded to or collected from customers through a rate recovery/refund methodology approved by the Commission at the time the Company files its annual reconciliation.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS109 Income Taxes - Electric Transmission Non-Plant:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the difference in the provision for income taxes for regulated non-plant liabilities recorded under ASC 980, which is based on amounts reflected in customer rates set by the relevant regulators, and the amount that is recorded under ASC740 for GAAP. This difference represents tax benefits previously flowed through to the customer that will be refunded from the customer to the Company through rates over an amortization period approved by the Commission. In addition, this difference primarily relates to deficient accumulated deferred income taxes resulting from the reduction of the income tax rate from 35% to 21% due to the enactment of the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS109 income taxes - Electric Distribution Non-Plant:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the difference in the provision for income taxes for regulated non-plant liabilities recorded under ASC 980, which is based on amounts reflected in customer rates set by the relevant regulators, and the amount that is recorded under ASC740 for GAAP. This difference represents tax benefits previously flowed through to the customer that will be refunded from the customer to the Company through rates over an amortization period approved by the Commission. In addition, this difference primarily relates to deficient accumulated deferred income taxes resulting from the reduction of the income tax rate from 35% to 21% due to the enactment of the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS109 income taxes - Gas Non-Plant:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the difference in the provision for income taxes for regulated non-plant liabilities recorded under ASC 980, which is based on amounts reflected in customer rates set by the relevant regulators, and the amount that is recorded under ASC740 for GAAP. This difference represents tax benefits previously flowed through to the customer that will be refunded from the customer to the Company through rates over an amortization period approved by the Commission. In addition, this difference primarily relates to deficient accumulated deferred income taxes resulting from the reduction of the income tax rate from 35% to 21% due to the enactment of the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Renewable Energy Credit Obligation:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4805, the Company procures Renewable Energy Certificates (RECs) throughout the year from third parties, as well as Internal Generation. The Company then submits the REC's it has procured to the state authorities once a year around June of each year for the previous calendar year&#8217;s obligation. The obligation amount is set by state regulators on an annual basis. In order to meet this obligation, Renewable Energy Certificates (RECs), for varying quantities of credits, are procured throughout the year from different sources.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Contract Termination Charge:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant R.I.P.U.C. No. 1188 (Docket No. 4599), Non-Bypassable Transition Charge Adjustment Provision, the Non-Bypassable Transition Charge shall collect from customers all Contract Termination Charges billed to the Narragansett Electric Company (the Company). The Non-Bypassable Transition Charge may be subject to adjustment each time any Contract Termination Charge changes. On an annual basis, the Company shall reconcile its total cost of Contract Termination Charges against its total transition charge revenue to determine any excess or deficiency. Any positive or negative balance will accrue interest calculated at the rate in effect for customer deposits.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Commodity &#8211; Admin:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4556, Standard Offer Adjustment Provision (&#8220;SOAP&#8221;) recovers administrative costs associated with arranging, administering, and providing SOS. In accordance with the SOAP, on an annual basis, the Company reconciles its administrative cost of providing SOS with its SOS revenue associated with the recovery of administrative costs, and the excess or deficiency, including interest at the interest rate paid on customer deposits, is refunded to, or recovered from, SOS customers in the subsequent year&#8217;s SOSACA Factor. Administrative costs include the cost of working capital, the administrative costs of complying with the requirements of Renewable Energy Standard, the costs of creating the environmental disclosure label, the costs associated with NEPOOL&#8217;s Generation Information System (&#8220;GIS&#8221;), the costs associated with the procurement of SOS including requests for bids, contract negotiation, and execution and contract administration, the costs associated with notifying SOS customers of the rates for SOS, the costs associated with updating rate changes in the Company&#8217;s billing system, and an allowance for SOS-related uncollectible expense associated with amounts billed through SOS rates and the SOSACA Factors.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Asset Retirement Obligation:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Regulated companies record under the ASC 980 FAS 71, depreciation, accretion expense and gain/loss resulting from ARO settlements. Under the ASC 980 the income statement impact is deferred to regulatory asset because all actual ARO costs are recovered in rates (through the COR component of the allowed depreciation rates), therefore, the Company may recognize an additional regulatory asset rather than a cumulative adjustment to the income statement.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Gas Futures - Gas Supply:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">The Company uses derivative instruments to manage commodity price risk. All commodity costs, including the impact of derivative instruments, are passed on to customers through the Company&#8217;s commodity rate adjustment mechanisms. A regulatory asset is recorded to defer the recognition of unrealized losses on derivative instruments. The gains or losses on the settlement of these contracts are recognized as purchased gas on the statement of income and then refunded to, or collected from, customers consistent with regulatory requirements.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Long-Term Contracting for Renewable Energy Resources:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 4599, in regards to Long-Term Contracting for Renewable Energy Recovery (LTCRER), the Company is required to enter into contracts with eligible renewable energy resources at fixed prices for the purchase of energy, capacity, and Renewable Energy Certificates (&#8220;RECs&#8221;). The Company sells the energy purchased through the contracts into the ISO-NE energy market and will use the RECS to satisfy the Company&#8217;s REC obligation associated with Standard Offer Services. The difference between the cost incurred under each contract and the proceeds that the Company receives for the sale of the Contract Products, is referred to as the above market contract cost. The above market contract costs are to be recovered from all retail delivery service customers through a uniform per kWh factor per the LTCRER Provision. In addition, the Company is authorized to recover 2.75 percent of the total payments made under each contract as remuneration. Certain administrative and other costs are tracked and recovered annually. A debit balance in the accounts represents an under recovery and a credit balance represents an over recovery of costs.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Net Metering Deferral:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 4599, the Company recovers through a Net Metering Charge the sum of 1) all Renewable Net Metering Credits (RNMCs) paid to eligible net metering customers, less any payments from ISONE for the sales of excess generation, and 2) the difference between the payments made to QFs with renewable generation at the SOS rate and the payments received from ISONE for market energy sold. The Net Metering Charge is a uniform per kWh charge applicable to all customers and is included with the distribution kWh charge for billing purposes. Company pays RNMCs to an Eligible Net Metering System (&#8220;Host Customer&#8221;) for up to one hundred percent (100%) of the Host Customer&#8217;s usage at the Eligible Net Metering System site. For kWh generated by both eligible renewable net metering customers and renewable QFs, the Company receives payments from ISO-NE for the sale of this energy in the market. These payments are used to offset the RNMCs paid to Host Customers and payments to renewable QFs.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">DAC - Reconciliation Factor:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4708, the DAC was established to provide for the recovery and reconciliation of the costs of identifiable special programs. Thus, the DAC is comprised of several factors relative to those specific programs. Each component reconciles the amount approved by the PUC for recovery or refund, for actual revenue billed through June 30th and forecasted revenue through October 1st and any true ups representing the actual revenue through June 30th and forecasted revenue through October 31st.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Renewable Energy Growth:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4626, Renewable Energy Growth Program Cost Recovery Provision permits for the recovery of incremental costs associated with the Renewable Energy (RE) Growth Program from all retail delivery service customers through a fixed monthly charge per customer. Pursuant to the RE Growth Statute, the Company is required to purchase the output generated by DG projects that have been awarded Certificates of Eligibility through the RE Growth Program enrollment process and to compensate program applicants in the form of PBI Payments. In turn, the applicants will assign to the Company title to all energy, capacity, and RECs (collectively, Market Products) generated by the DG projects.5 The Company will sell the energy and the RECs as required by the RE Growth Statute. The net proceeds from the sale of the Market Products will be used to offset the cost of the RE Growth Program. In addition, the RE Growth Statute authorizes the Company to recover 1.75 percent of the total PBI Payments as remuneration. Certain implementation, administrative and other costs authorized through various sections of the RE Growth Statute will be tracked and recovered annually.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Arrears Forgiveness &#8211; LAMP:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utility Commission Docket No. 4770, beginning July 1, 2018, the Company will be able to recover arrearages forgiven for those customers who participate in the Arrearage Management program that have exceeded the allowable bad debt amount for the twelve-month period ending December 31, 2017. The recovery will be effected by the Arrearage Management Allowance Factor added to the Delivery Service rates and collected through a distribution per kilowatt-hour (kWh) charge on customers&#8217; bills.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Low Income Discount Recovery Factor</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4770 (Rate Case) - The Settlement Agreement provides that the Company shall develop and implement a Low-Income Discount Recovery Factor. The Settlement Agreement also provides for additional benefits for Low-Income Discount customers receive service on Rate A-60. Customers receiving service on the A-60 rate shall receive a total bill discount of 25% off the total amount billed. Additionally, for customers receiving benefits through Medicaid, General Public Assistance, and/or the Family Independence Program, an additional discount of 5% off of the total amount billed. Customers billed on the Rate A-60, shall not be assessed the Low Income Discount Recovery Factor. The Company will also report on the enrollment in the low-income discount program, represented by a number of customers receiving delivery service on Rate A-60, for the purposes of developing a baseline and eventually setting an enrollment target that improves upon the baseline. Such a target would incentivize expanded enrollment on Rate A-60 thus increasing stability of service and reducing the frequency of the termination/reconnection cycle.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Storage Deferral:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">The Company defers the hedge loss associated with the storage for gas procurement. The objective is to mitigate the volatility of natural gas costs and to encourage the Company to achieve lower hedged commodity costs for customers. Under this program, the Company locks, or hedges, the variable cost of the natural gas commodity exposure.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Performance Incentive:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-bottom:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 4770, the Incentive is intended to advance state policy goals and drive benefits for its customers. The company estimated that the incentive could create substantial annual and lifetime savings for Rhode Island customers base on megawatts savings. To quantify the incentive, a metric was created that includes a minimum, a mid, and a maximum target, that the company can earn with an increasing earning opportunity at each level. This metric is intended to reflect avoided capacity. If the Company meets its target, it can earn an incentive for the three rate years August 2019 through 2021. The company will file a report annually due March 1st for the previous year&#8217;s performance incentive to be approved by the RIPUC. Incentive will be recorded as a deferral and will be collected beginning July 1st through a factor.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Net Storm Revenue Credit:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">The Storm Net Revenue Factor is designed to credit back customers for the value of services performed by the Company's employees in other jurisdiction, including outside of the Company's operating companies service territories in accordance with the provisions Compliance attachment 23 of the Amended Settlement Agreement in RIPUC Docket No. 4770. The Storm Net Revenue is defined as the proceeds received or cost reductions achieved for base labor and non-incremental labor overhead costs on all labor (i.e., not just base labor) charged for storm restoration services provided to other utilities, whether affiliated or non-affiliated, less an amount equal to percentage, which is the labor capitalization rate set in the Company&#8217;s general rate case. Beginning November 1st, 2022 through October 31st, 2023, RIE will return 75% of the proceeds from storms revenue back to customers through the DAC.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Transmission Deferral:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 4599, The Company records the over or under recovery of transmission expenses from Independent System Operators - New England (ISO - NE) and New England Power (NEP), and any other transmission service provider that is authorized to bill the Company directly for transmission services. Transmission charges are determined annually based upon a forecast of transmission expense for the upcoming year and a transmission adjustment factor which is designed to recover from or refund to customers under or over recoveries of expense from the prior year. The Company is also allowed to recover an allowance for uncollectible expense associated with amounts billed through the transmission charges at the rate approved by the Commission.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Waived Electronic Transaction Fees:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 24-05-GE, the Company is to record a regulatory asset related to the recovery of credit card, debit card, and related fees (collectively, "Electronic Transaction Fees") that Rhode Island Energy has paid and will continue to pay on behalf of its electric and gas customers pursuant to the PUC's Orders issued in Docket No. 5022.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">ISR &#8211; Electric:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 4995, the ISR Plan itemizes the recommended work activities by general category and provides budgets for capital investment. After the end of the fiscal year the Company would true up the ISR Plan&#8217;s projected capital used for establishing the revenue requirement to actual or allowed investment and expenditures on a cumulative basis and reconcile the revenue requirement associated with the actual investment and expenditures to the revenue billed from the rate adjustments implemented at the beginning of each fiscal year. Capital investments are recovered through a capital rate adjustment mechanism that reconciles the plant in service amounts associated with this projected spending to the lesser of actual plant in service or actual spending on a cumulative basis following the close of the fiscal year.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Rate Case Expenses</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket #4770,the rate case expenses consist of work necessary for compliance in filing: capital analysis, depreciation study, decoupling proposal, revenue requirement and rate design, and legal expenses and miscellaneous expenses.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Mitigation of Winter Price Volitility</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket 25-28-EL, this proceeding was established to implement a winter price bill credit mechanism to mitigate the impact of elevated electric supply during the winter period. Under this mechanism, bill credits are applied to eligible customer accounts to provide targeted bill relief. The associated costs of the credits are deferred and subsequently recovered through an approved rate adjustment mechanism. The docket includes the calculation, allocation, and reconciliation of bill credits to ensure accurate cost recovery and that resulting rates remain just and reasonable.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Storm Deferral</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 2509, The Company&#8217;s Storm Fund was established to provide a mechanism for recovering storm restoration expenses as a result of extraordinary storms without the need to file for rate surcharges or periodic rate relief. For any storm for which the Company incurs incremental operation and maintenance (O&amp;M) storm-related costs above the applicable dollar threshold amount, the Company is authorized to charge the Storm Fund for these expense amounts above the per-storm deductible amount.</span>
      </div>
    </link:footnote>
    <link:footnote xlink:type="resource" xlink:label="fn-9" id="fn-9" xml:lang="en" xlink:role="http://www.xbrl.org/2003/role/footnote">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">The information below includes the rate order or document number and the period of amortization, if applicable, for each regulatory liability listed on page 278.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">LIHEAP Enhancement Program &#8211; Electric:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4290, R.I.P.U.C. No. 2143, and in accordance with R.I.G.L. &#167;39-1-27.12, the Company bills to all customers a LIHEAP Enhancement Plan charge approved by the Commission. A LIHEAP Enhancement charge fund is used to account for the combined funds collected through the LIHEAP Enhancement charge from both gas and electric service customers. The State Office of Energy Resource designates to the Company the qualifying customer accounts and the amounts to be credited from the LIHEAP Enhancement fund. The cumulative annual amount of credits applied to customer bills will be limited to an amount no greater than the cumulative aggregate projected LIHEAP Enhancement Plan Charge billed through the end of the current calendar year. Once the aggregate credits applied to customers bills equals the aggregate projected Enhancement Plan charge billed during the calendar year, including interest at the customer deposit interest rate, the Enhancement Plan credits would cease.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Residential Assistance Adj Factor</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4770 (Rate Case) - The Settlement Agreement provides that the Company shall develop and implement a Low-Income Discount Recovery Factor. The Settlement Agreement also provides for additional benefits for Low-Income Discount customers receive service on Rate A-60. Customers receiving service on the A-60 rate shall receive a total bill discount of 25% off the total amount billed. Additionally, for customers receiving benefits through Medicaid, General Public Assistance, and/or the Family Independence Program, an additional discount of 5% off of the total amount billed. Customers billed on the Rate A-60, shall not be assessed the Low Income Discount Recovery Factor. The Company will also report on the enrollment in the low-income discount program, represented by a number of customers receiving delivery service on Rate A-60, for the purposes of developing a baseline and eventually setting an enrollment target that improves upon the baseline. Such a target would incentivise expanded enrollment on Rate A-60 thus increasing stability of service and reducing the frequency of the termination/reconnection cycle.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Low Income Program</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4770 (Rate Case) - The Settlement Agreement provides that the Company shall develop and implement a Low-Income Discount Recovery Factor. The Settlement Agreement also provides for additional benefits for Low-Income Discount customers receive service on Rate A-60. Customers receiving service on the A-60 rate shall receive a total bill discount of 25% off the total amount billed. Additionally, for customers receiving benefits through Medicaid, General Public Assistance, and/or the Family Independence Program, an additional discount of 5% off of the total amount billed. Customers billed on the Rate A-60, shall not be assessed the Low Income Discount Recovery Factor. The Company will also report on the enrollment in the low-income discount program, represented by a number of customers receiving delivery service on Rate A-60, for the purposes of developing a baseline and eventually setting an enrollment target that improves upon the baseline. Such a target would incentivise expanded enrollment on Rate A-60 thus increasing stability of service and reducing the frequency of the termination/reconnection cycle.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Deferred Revenue-Gas Cost Recovery</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4719, the Company is authorized to recover its cost of providing gas supply through annual filings, which request Gas Cost Recovery (GCR) factors applicable to firm sales customers. The GCR includes all costs of firm gas, including, but not limited to, commodity costs, demand charges, hedging and hedging related costs, local production and storage costs and other gas supply expense incurred to procure and transport supplies, transportation fees, inventory finance costs, requirements for purchased gas working capital, all applicable credits, taxes, deferred gas costs and commodity-related bad debt.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS109 Income Taxes - Electric Transmission Plant:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the difference in the provision for income taxes for regulated plant assets recorded under ASC 980, which is based on amounts reflected in customer rates set by the relevant regulators, and the amount that is recorded under ASC740 for GAAP. This difference represents tax expense previously funded by the customer that will be returned to the customer through rates over an amortization period approved by the Commission. In addition, this difference primarily relates to excess accumulated deferred income taxes resulting from the reduction of the income tax rate from 35% to 21% due to the enactment of the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS109 Income Taxes - Electric Distribution Plant</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the difference in the provision for income taxes for regulated plant assets recorded under ASC 980, which is based on amounts reflected in customer rates set by the relevant regulators, and the amount that is recorded under ASC740 for GAAP. This difference represents tax expense previously funded by the customer that will be returned to the customer through rates over an amortization period approved by the Commission. In addition, this difference primarily relates to excess accumulated deferred income taxes resulting from the reduction of the income tax rate from 35% to 21% due to the enactment of the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS109 Income Taxes - Gas Plant:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the difference in the provision for income taxes for regulated plant assets recorded under ASC 980, which is based on amounts reflected in customer rates set by the relevant regulators, and the amount that is recorded under ASC740 for GAAP. This difference represents tax expense previously funded by the customer that will be returned to the customer through rates over an amortization period approved by the Commission. In addition, this difference primarily relates to excess accumulated deferred income taxes resulting from the reduction of the income tax rate from 35% to 21% due to the enactment of the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS109 Income Taxes - Electric Transmission Non-Plant</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the difference in the provision for income taxes for regulated non-plant assets recorded under ASC 980, which is based on amounts reflected in customer rates set by the relevant regulators, and the amount that is recorded under ASC740 for GAAP. This difference represents tax expense previously funded by the customer that will be returned to the customer through rates over an amortization period approved by the Commission. In addition, this difference primarily relates to excess accumulated deferred income taxes resulting from the reduction of the income tax rate from 35% to 21% due to the enactment of the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS109 Income Taxes - Electric Distribution Non-Plant</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the difference in the provision for income taxes for regulated non-plant assets recorded under ASC 980, which is based on amounts reflected in customer rates set by the relevant regulators, and the amount that is recorded under ASC740 for GAAP. This difference represents tax expense previously funded by the customer that will be returned to the customer through rates over an amortization period approved by the Commission. In addition, this difference primarily relates to excess accumulated deferred income taxes resulting from the reduction of the income tax rate from 35% to 21% due to the enactment of the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS109 Income Taxes - Gas Non-Plant</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the difference in the provision for income taxes for regulated non-plant assets recorded under ASC 980, which is based on amounts reflected in customer rates set by the relevant regulators, and the amount that is recorded under ASC740 for GAAP. This difference represents tax expense previously funded by the customer that will be returned to the customer through rates over an amortization period approved by the Commission. In addition, this difference primarily relates to excess accumulated deferred income taxes resulting from the reduction of the income tax rate from 35% to 21% due to the enactment of the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">ISR Deferral - Gas:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">The Gas Infrastructure, Safety, and Reliability (ISR) Plan includes capital investment spending needed to meet state and federal regulatory requirements applicable to the Company&#8217;s gas system and to maintain its distribution infrastructure in a safe and reliable condition.&#160; Each year the Company proposes a plan for the upcoming fiscal year, where it itemizes the recommended work activities by general category and provides budgets for capital investment and O&amp;M. Recovery begins April 1 for costs associated with that fiscal year. After the end of the fiscal year the Company reconciles the ISR Plan&#8217;s projected capital used and O&amp;M estimates for establishing the revenue requirement to actual investment and O&amp;M expenditures and reconciles the actual revenue requirement to the revenue billed from the factors implemented at the beginning of each fiscal year.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Customer Credit Refund Adjustment</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to terms of the Distribution Rate Plan, Second Amended Stipulation and Settlement dated October 15, 2004 (the &#8220;Docket 3617 Settlement&#8221;), effective November 1, 2004, approved by the Commission in Order No. 18037, the Company began refunding to its distribution customers, over a twelve-month period, a net credit estimated at $22,769,000. This net credit related to customers&#8217; share of shared earnings, exogenous event impacts and low-income expansion lost revenue offsets pursuant to terms of the Company&#8217;s Third Amended Stipulation and Agreement along with accrued service quality penalties for the years 2000 through 2003. The Customer Credit adjustment factor that otherwise would have been allocated to the Company&#8217;s customers in the Cities of Providence and East Providence (will be retained by the Company and used to support the undergrounding of the Company&#8217;s E-183 transmission line).</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Environmental Response Fund</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 2162, the Company is required to defer the Environmental Response Costs, which is defined as all the reasonable and prudently incurred costs associated with remedial and clean-up obligations of Rhode Island Energy arising out of the Company's operation of manufactured gas plants and sites associated with the operation and disposal activities from such gas plants, and electric operations of the Company or its predecessor companies that gave rise to deposits or waste, which are regulated under the Comprehensive Environmental Response. Environmental Response Costs also include costs of acquiring property associated with the clean-up of such sites as well as litigation costs, claims, judgements and settlements associated with such sites.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Hold Harmless</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents Hold Harmless relief available to customers to mitigate winter rate increases.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Service Quality Penalties</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 3628, The Plan provides for penalties and offsets relating to performance standards in the areas of reliability and customer service. Under the Reliability Performance Standards, an interruption is defined as the loss of electric service to more than one customer for more than one minute. The interruption duration is defined as the period of time, measured in minutes, from the initial notification of the interruption event to the time when service has been restored to the customers. Under the Customer Service Standards, the customer contact survey results are based on responses from the Company's customers. Eight types of transactions are included in the survey, and the overall results are weighted based on the number of these transactions actually performed at the call center during the calendar year.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Incentive Earnings &#8211; NGPMP</span>
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 4323, the Company is under agreement to return excess earnings back to customers. Excess earnings is defined as earnings over sharing threshold. Sharing threshold is the allowed ROE plus the maximum EE performance Incentive earnings potential in the calendar year (in basis points) plus the maximum performance incentives.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Storm Deferral</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 2509, The Company&#8217;s Storm Fund was established to provide a mechanism for recovering storm restoration expenses as a result of extraordinary storms without the need to file for rate surcharges or periodic rate relief. For any storm for which the Company incurs incremental operation and maintenance (O&amp;M) storm-related costs above the applicable dollar threshold amount, the Company is authorized to charge the Storm Fund for these expense amounts above the per-storm deductible amount.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Mitigation of Winter Price Volatility</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 25-28-EL, The Company is ordered to issue miscellaneous bill credits for all residential electric customers for the months of January, February and March 2026.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Revenue Decoupling - Gas</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4634, the Distribution Adjustment Clause (DAC) was established to provide for the recovery of the costs of identifiable special programs. The DAC provision includes several components annual System Pressure (SP) factor; an Advanced Gas Technology (AGT) factor; a Low Income Assistance Program (LIAP) factor, an Environmental Response Cost (ERC) factor; a Pension Adjustment factor (PAF); an On-System Margon Credit (MC) factor; a Service Quality Performance (SQP) factor; a Revenue Decoupling Adjustment (RDA) factor; rate class specific Infrastructure, Safety, and Reliability (ISR) factors; an Earnings Sharing Mechanism (ESM) factor; a Firm Revenue Credit factor; and the Reconciliation (R) factors for the previous year's DAC factors. The Revenue Decoupling Mechanism (RDM) provides for an annual reconciliation of actual base revenue-per-customer by rate class against benchmark revenue-per-customer.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Gas CoPay EE</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">In pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 4755, the company issues incentives from the programs to customers to move them to install energy efficient equipment. Incentives include, but are not limited to, rebates to customers, copayments to vendors for direct installation of measures, payments to distributors to buy down the cost of their products for sale in retail stores, payments to vendors to create and deliver information, the cost of an education course, or payments to lenders to buy down the interest in a loan.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">LCI CoPay EE</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">In pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 4755, the company issues incentives from the programs to customers to move them to install energy efficient equipment. Incentives include, but are not limited to, rebates to customers, copayments to vendors for direct installation of measures, payments to distributors to buy down the cost of their products for sale in retail stores, payments to vendors to create and deliver information, the cost of an education course, or payments to lenders to buy down the interest in a loan.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Fed Tax Pay-Gen ITC</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents deferred taxes on the book deferral of investment tax credits ("ITC"). ITC amortizes over the average useful book life of gas utility property to which the credits relate. As customers receive the tax benefit of ITC amortization, the deferred taxes reverse.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Pension/OPEB Carrying Charges- Electric</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943 and 4223, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the pension &amp; PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference. The balance reflects the remaining unamortized net pension &amp; PBOP gains from National Grids' acquisition of Narragansett Electric.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Pension/OPEB Carrying Charges- Gas</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943 and 4223, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the pension &amp; PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference. The balance reflects the remaining unamortized net pension &amp; PBOP gains from National Grids' acquisition of Narragansett Electric.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">OPEB Expense Deferred - Electric</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">OPEB Expense Deferred - Gas</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS158-PBOP - Electric</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS158-PBOP - Gas</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Pension PAM Deferral - Electric</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Pension PAM Deferral - Gas</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS158 - Pension - Electric(PSC)</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS158 - Pension - Gas(PSC)</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS158 - OPEB - Electric(PSC)</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">FAS158 - OPEB - Gas(PSC)</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 3943, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s proposal to reconcile its pension and postretirement benefits other than pensions (&#8220;PBOP&#8221;) expenses annually through the DAC. In accordance with the Company&#8217;s Tariff, RIPUC NG No. 101, the PBOP adjustment factor is designed to recover or refund the prior year&#8217;s reconciliation of the Company&#8217;s actual pension and PBOP expenses to the Company&#8217;s pension and PBOP expenses that were included in base rates. The adjustment factor is based on this difference.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">DAC - Unbilled Gas</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Utility Commission, the Company is required to track the monthly Unbilled Revenue applicable to the Distribution Adjustment Charge (DAC) and the applicable Gross Earnings Tax (GET). Increases in this account comprise of current month unbilled accruals and decreases are prior month's unbilled accrual reversal. The balance in this account represents the current month's unbilled revenue for DAC and the applicable GET.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Power Sector Transformation</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">According to Docket Nos. 4770/4780, the Company is required by the Public Utility Commission of Rhode Island to institute a Power Sector Transformation (PST) Vision and Implementation Plan (PST Plan) within the Rate Plan to modernize the electric distribution system and enable technologies that will reduce greenhouse gas emissions and help to control costs for customers. PST Plan sets forth five key 14 components and the associated cost recovery, including a calculation of associated 15 revenue requirements and a Performance Incentive Mechanism proposal. These components are all lumped into one regulatory liability account. The Company is proposing a PST Provision for recovery of the costs associated with the PST Plan. The PST factors will allow for recovery of the cumulative revenue requirement as approved by the PUC in the Company&#8217;s annual PST filings and will be applicable for the twelve month period commencing on April 1 of each year.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">DAC - Reconciliation Factor</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4708, the DAC was established to provide for the recovery and reconciliation of the costs of identifiable special programs. Thus, the DAC is comprised of several factors relative to those specific programs. Each component reconciles the amount approved by the PUC for recovery or refund, for actual revenue billed through June 30th and forecasted revenue through October 1st and any true ups representing the actual revenue through June 30th and forecasted revenue through October 31st.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">DAC - System Pressure</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4290, R.I.P.U.C. No. 2143, the Company is required to maintaining proper operating pressure on the Company&#8217;s distribution system requires the occasional use of the Company&#8217;s liquefied natural gas ("LNG") facilities. The System Pressure cost included in the DAC represents projected LNG demand costs for the period November 1, through October 31 utilized to ensure proper operating pressure along the Company&#8217;s distribution system. The System Pressure balancing percentage used to determine the cost to be reflected in the DAC is 100.00% of the projected LNG lease payments for the fiscal year.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Renewable Energy Standard</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Docket No. 4930, Standard Offer Adjustment Provision, the Renewable Energy Standard ("RES") is designed to recover from customers the estimated costs associated with the upcoming RES obligation year and an estimate of the remaining costs for the current RES obligation year. The estimate of the remaining costs for the current RES obligation year is based on a reconciliation of actual RES revenue and actual RES expense for the current year and an estimate of remaining RES expense to satisfy the obligation year. The expected cost of Renewable Energy Certificates (&#8220;RECs&#8221;) to be procured for current and upcoming obligation years is based on the most recently available market data and broker sheets.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Energy Efficiency</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission, Docket No. 4654, The Energy Efficiency programs offers energy efficiency opportunities to all customer segments, with a focus on providing needed services to low and moderate income residential consumers as a means of reducing bills. These programs include the EnergyWise Program, the Single Family Low Income Services Program, and the ENERGY STAR Homes Program. The objectives of the plan are: 1. To create economic value and cost savings for Rhode Islanders. 2. To ensure all customers have an opportunity to participate in energy efficiency programs. 3. To achieve electric savings targets established in the Least Cost Procurement Plan (LCPP), approved by the Commission in Docket 3931. 4. To develop the infrastructure needed to meet the EERMC&#8217;s proposed performance targets. and 5. Innovation - The Company&#8217;s residential pilots provided the foundation for innovation by testing new products like solar thermal hot water and heating for gas systems, ECM pump motors, Wi-fi thermostats, heat pump water heaters and boiler load controls. In accordance with R.I.G.L. &#167; 39-1-27.7 and 39-2-1.2, a charge per dekatherm (Dt) is designed to recover the costs of the Company&#8217;s gas Energy Efficiency Program (EEP).</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Transmission Formula Rate</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the over collection of the Company's Transmission Formula Rate, as set forth by the FERC. This amount is classified in Account 182.3 when the balance in the account is a net asset, or in Account 254 when the balance in the account is a net liability.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 25-36-EL, the Company's Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative (&#8220;RGGI&#8221;) Auction Proceeds Customer Bill Credit For A-60 Rate Class - RGGI 2025-A &amp; RGGI 2025B Plan. The purpose of this filing is to add a tariff to govern the utilization of RGGI auction proceeds to provide bill credits to customers in the A-60 rate class. If approved, customers on the A-60 rate class will receive a bill credit of $46.51 in December 2025, a second bill credit of $46.51 in January 2026, and third bill credit of $46.51 in February 2026. The RGGI funds were made available to the Company by the Rhode Island Office of Energy Resources through Section 5.4.2.b. of the RGGI 2025-A Plan and Section 5.6.2.b. of the RGGI 2025-B Plan.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Demand Response Pilot</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Represents the over collection of the Company's Gas Demand Response Pilot program as set forth by the Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 23-46-EE, to test customer adoption and the effectiveness of gas demand response in reducing system peak demand during the period 2024-2026.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">EE ConnectedSolutions</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 24-06-EE, the Company was directed to file an System Reliability Procurement ("SRP") factor that is designed to collect an amount equal to the Three-Year Budget, plus the applicable regulatory allocation calculated for OER, over a 29-month period, commencing for usage on and after August 1, 2024.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Renewable Energy Growth</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission Docket No. 4626, Renewable Energy Growth Program Cost Recovery Provision permits for the recovery of incremental costs associated with the Renewable Energy (RE) Growth Program from all retail delivery service customers through a fixed monthly charge per customer. Pursuant to the RE Growth Statute, the Company is required to purchase the output generated by DG projects that have been awarded Certificates of Eligibility through the RE Growth Program enrollment process and to compensate program applicants in the form of PBI Payments. In turn, the applicants will assign to the Company title to all energy, capacity, and RECs (collectively, Market Products) generated by the DG projects.5 The Company will sell the energy and the RECs as required by the RE Growth Statute. The net proceeds from the sale of the Market Products will be used to offset the cost of the RE Growth Program. In addition, the RE Growth Statute authorizes the Company to recover 1.75 percent of the total PBI Payments as remuneration. Certain implementation, administrative and other costs authorized through various sections of the RE Growth Statute will be tracked and recovered annually.</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="margin-top:14pt">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%">Revenue Decoupling - Electric</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%">Pursuant to Rhode Island Public Utilities Commission in Docket No. 4617, Company is required to submit its annual Revenue Decoupling Mechanism (RDM) revenue reconciliation by May 15 of each year. The reconciliation compares the Annual Target Revenue to actual billed distribution revenue for the RDM year. The Revenue Decoupling amount is the difference (either positive or negative) between the actual billed distribution revenue and the ATR for the RDM Year. The RDM Deferral Amount (either positive or negative), including interest at the rate paid on customer deposits, determines the RDM Adjustment Factor. The RDM Adjustment Factor is a uniform per kWh charge applicable to all customers distribution rates, such as customer charges, distribution energy charges, distribution demand charges, high voltage metering and delivery credits under the Company&#8217;s general service rate classes, paperless bill credit, and luminaire and pole charges under the Company&#8217;s street and area lighting rates.</span>
      </div>
    </link:footnote>
    <link:footnote xlink:type="resource" xlink:label="fn-10" id="fn-10" xml:lang="en" xlink:role="http://www.xbrl.org/2003/role/footnote">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
